-- Q2955: Financial Statement Analysis [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The divergence indicates accounting fraud because a profitable company cannot legitimately have negative free cash flow","The divergence is meaningless because net income and free cash flow measure entirely different concepts with no analytical relationship","The divergence indicates that capital expenditures and working capital investments exceeded cash-generating capacity, raising questions about earnings sustainability","The divergence indicates the company is paying excessive dividends that drain cash while net income remains positive on the income statement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 230-10-45, FCFF equals operating cash flow minus capital expenditures adjusted for taxes. Pinnacle''s manufacturing expansion and new product launches explain the large capex outlays that exceed cash generation. While growth-stage companies routinely report this pattern, persistent negative FCFF despite positive accrual earnings may require external financing.
Wrong (A): Profitable companies frequently have negative free cash flow during heavy investment periods; this is a normal pattern, not evidence of fraud.
Wrong (B): Net income and free cash flow are analytically related; their divergence provides critical insight into earnings quality and capital allocation.
Wrong (D): Dividends are paid from cash but are not deducted in calculating FCFF, so they do not explain the divergence between net income and free cash flow.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2955;

