  choices = $EXPL$["It uses net income rather than operating income as the numerator in the coverage calculation","It measures only long-term solvency and ignores short-term debt service capacity entirely","It excludes depreciation and amortization from the numerator of the coverage calculation","It includes lease payments and other fixed obligations in the denominator beyond just interest expense"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 842 and general financial analysis standards, the fixed charge coverage ratio expands the denominator beyond interest expense to include all fixed obligations such as lease payments and preferred dividends. This provides a more comprehensive measure of a company''s ability to meet its total fixed commitments than times interest earned.
Wrong (A): Both ratios use earnings before interest and taxes (or a variant) as the numerator, not net income; the key difference is in the denominator.
Wrong (B): The fixed charge coverage ratio measures the ability to meet all near-term fixed obligations, not exclusively long-term solvency.
Wrong (C): The fixed charge coverage ratio does not exclude depreciation and amortization; its distinguishing feature is the broader denominator, not numerator adjustments.$EXPL$
