  choices = $EXPL$["Automation lowers the breakeven point from 20,000 to 37,500 units and reduces operating leverage, decreasing both risk and reward potential","Automation raises the breakeven point from 20,000 to 37,500 units but has no effect on operating leverage since leverage depends only on debt","Automation raises the breakeven point from 20,000 to 37,500 units and increases operating leverage, creating higher risk below breakeven but greater profit potential above it","Automation eliminates the breakeven point entirely because the lower variable cost per unit guarantees profitability at any production volume"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per CVP analysis principles, before automation the contribution margin is $20/unit ($50 - $30) with breakeven at 20,000 units ($400,000 / $20). After automation, the contribution margin rises to $32/unit ($50 - $18) but breakeven increases to 37,500 units ($1,200,000 / $32). The higher fixed cost proportion increases the degree of operating leverage, amplifying both upside and downside.
Wrong (A): Automation raises, not lowers, the breakeven point because fixed costs tripled from $400,000 to $1,200,000.
Wrong (B): Operating leverage is determined by the fixed-to-variable cost ratio, not by debt levels; financial leverage relates to debt.
Wrong (D): A breakeven point always exists when fixed costs are positive; lower variable costs do not guarantee profitability at all volumes.$EXPL$
